.TH SHIMBACK 1 "2026-09-26" "shimback 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shimback \- run a primary command, transparently fall back to another
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shimback add
.I name
[\fB\-s\fR \fIsource\fR] [\fB\-\-source\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|.
\fB\-f\fR \fIfallback\fR [\fB\-\-fallback\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIexit\-code\fR|\fIheuristic\fR|\fIexit\-code\-match\fR|\fIroute\-args\fR|\fIrewrite\fR|\fIsplit\-args\fR|\fIroute\-map\fR|\fIpassthrough\fR]
[\fB\-\-error\-pattern\fR \fIp\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR \fIcode\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-route\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-strip\-matched\-args\fR]
[\fB\-\-split\-source\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-split\-fallback\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-rewrite\fR \fIfrom\fR=\fIto\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-route\fR \fImatch\fR=\fIcommand\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-diagnostic\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
[\fB\-\-capture\-timeout\fR \fIms\fR] [\fB\-\-capture\-limit\fR \fIsize\fR]
.br
.B shimback remove
[\fB\-y\fR]
.I name
(alias:
.BR rm )
.br
.B shimback init
.br
.B shimback list
[\fB\-\-full\fR]
(alias:
.BR ls )
.br
.B shimback doctor
[\fBfix\fR]
.br
.B shimback install
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-\-shell\fR \fIshell\fR[,\fIshell\fR]\|.\|.\|. | \fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B shimback update
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
.br
.B shimback uninstall
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fB\-\-full\fR]
.br
.B shimback edit
[\fIname\fR]
.br
.B shimback info
.I name
.br
.B shimback \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shimback
is a small, dependency\-free command\-line shim: it wraps a command name
(e.g.
.IR sed )
with a
.B source
binary to run and a
.B fallback
binary to transparently retry with if the source doesn't work out.
.PP
.B add
creates a symlink named
.I name
pointing at the
.B shimback
binary itself, inside a dedicated shim directory that's prepended to
.BR PATH .
When
.I name
is run, the OS finds that symlink first;
.B shimback
inspects
.IR argv[0] ,
sees it was invoked as
.I name
rather than
.BR shimback ,
looks up
.IR name 's
configuration, and runs the source command with the given arguments. If the
source command fails (the exact condition depends on the shim's policy, see
.B POLICIES
below),
.B shimback
transparently re\-runs the same arguments against the fallback command
instead. If the source succeeds, its output is passed through as if
.B shimback
weren't there at all.
.PP
A failed trial run of the source command is designed to be invisible: its
stdout and stderr are captured, not streamed live, and are discarded
entirely if a fallback is triggered \(em nothing about the failed attempt
reaches the terminal unless the shim explicitly opts into a one\-line
diagnostic (see
.B \-\-diagnostic
below).
.PP
This targets fast\-failing commands, which almost always fail in well under
a second with a small amount of output. Since there's no way to know
whether to show or hide a run's output before it exits, capturing has to
hold it in memory until then; to keep that bounded, a trial run that takes
too long or produces too much output \(em whichever happens first \(em
makes shimback give up on ever hiding or falling back on it, flush what was
captured so far, and relay the rest live instead. Nothing already produced
is ever lost; only the opportunity to fall back for that one invocation is,
since some of the source's real output is already visible by then. See
.B \-\-capture\-timeout
/
.B \-\-capture\-limit
below (default 2000ms / 8MiB, each also settable as a global default in the
config \(em see
.B FILES
below).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "add " name " [\-s " source "] \-f " fallback " [options...]"
Create or update a shim named
.IR name ,
wrapping
.I source
(or, if
.B \-s
is omitted, whatever
.I name
resolves to fresh on
.B PATH
at dispatch time, skipping shimback's own shim directory) with
.I fallback
under the given policy.
.B \-f
is required for every policy except
.I rewrite
and
.IR route\-map ,
neither of which ever uses a fallback at all. Both
.B \-s
and
.B \-f
accept a path or a bare command name (looked up on
.B PATH
once, at
.B add
time, the same way a shell would find it); either way the resolved absolute
path is what gets frozen into the config. Refuses to create a shim where
source and fallback resolve to the same binary with the same extra
arguments (same binary with
.B different
.BR \-\-source\-arg / \-\-fallback\-arg
is fine \(em two distinct invocations of one command, not a no\-op), or
where either resolves back to the
.B shimback
binary itself (a cycle \(em since every shim symlink points at the same
binary, this also catches a bare name that happens to resolve to another
shim). Also ensures the shim directory is on
.B PATH
for the current shell, via an idempotent marker\-block injection into its
startup file (on zsh,
.I ~/.zshrc.local
instead of
.I ~/.zshrc
when the former exists, since that's what most zsh setups source for
machine\-local overrides).
.IP
.B \-\-force
allows
.B \-s
/
.B \-f
to name a path (containing
.IR / ,
not a bare command name \(em there's nothing to search
.B PATH
for otherwise) that doesn't exist yet, instead of failing. Stored per\-shim
in the config as
.IR "force = true" ;
once set,
.B doctor
skips its existence check for whichever of source/fallback is still
missing, going back to checking it normally (cycle check included) as soon
as it actually exists.
.B force
affects only that
.B doctor
check \(em dispatch always resolves and checks source/fallback for real
every time the shim actually runs, regardless of it.
.IP
The confirmation line printed on success is colored when stdout is a
terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, matching
.BR list 's
conventions. The shell\-startup\-file notices right after it (
.IR "zsh: PATH updated in ..." ,
.IR "Restart your shell ..." )
are silent by default \(em pass
.B \-v
/
.B \-\-verbose
to print them for this one invocation, or set
.I "verbose = true"
at the top level of the config to make that the default for every
.B add
(see
.B FILES
below).
.B \-v
/
.B \-\-verbose
only ever turns them on; it cannot override a config default of
.I true
back to silent for one call.
.IP
.B \-\-capture\-timeout
.I ms
and
.B \-\-capture\-limit
.I size
override, for this shim only, the two thresholds deciding when a trial run
gives up on being hideable (see
.B DESCRIPTION
above): default 2000ms / 8MiB, both also settable as global defaults at the
top level of the config (see
.BR FILES ).
A shim\-level value always wins over the global one.
.B \-\-capture\-limit
accepts a plain byte count or a size with a case\-insensitive unit suffix
\(em
.BR B
(or nothing) for bytes,
.B K
/
.B KB
for decimal kilobytes (\(mu1000),
.B KiB
for binary kibibytes (\(mu1024), and likewise
.BR M / MB / MiB
and
.BR G / GB / GiB
\(em so
.IR 8MiB ,
.IR 8192KiB ,
and
.I 8388608
all mean the same thing.
.IP
.B \-\-source\-arg
and
.B \-\-fallback\-arg
(each repeatable) attach fixed, baked\-in arguments to source/fallback,
independent of policy and of each other \(em whichever one actually runs
gets its own extra arguments prepended before whatever the shim was
invoked with, every time. This lets a shim double as a regular alias with
flags built in, e.g.
.B \-\-source\-arg\ \-l\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-t\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-r\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-a\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-h
on a shim wrapping
.IR ls ,
the same shape as a shell alias like
.IR "alias cools='ls -ltrah'" .
Under
.IR rewrite ,
.B \-\-source\-arg
values land before the (possibly rewritten) invocation arguments, and are
never themselves subject to rewriting.
.B \-\-fallback\-arg
has nothing to attach to without a fallback (only possible under
.I rewrite
or
.IR route\-map ,
the two policies where
.B \-f
is optional) \(em given without one, it's discarded with a yellow warning
rather than kept around uselessly. The interactive wizard (below) goes a
step further and never offers the fallback\-args page at all when
fallback is left blank.
.IP
Run at an interactive terminal with required information missing (no
.IR name ,
or no
.IR fallback ,
or a policy missing the option it needs), an interactive wizard fills it
in instead of failing: one page per field (including
.B \-\-source\-arg
/
.B \-\-fallback\-arg
as their own optional list pages), Enter to confirm and continue
(skipping an optional one if left blank), Left/Right arrows to revisit
and edit earlier pages (Right only as far as a page already reached),
Esc or Ctrl\-C to abort without writing anything. Whatever was already
given on the command line is skipped straight to the first missing page,
but stays reachable via Left.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback add sed \-f /usr/bin/sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "remove [\-y] " name
Remove
.IR name 's
symlink and config entry. Also available as
.BR rm .
Leaves the
.B PATH
injection in the shell startup file alone, since other shims (or a future
.BR add )
may still need it. If
.I name
doesn't match any configured shim, prints the error first, then, right
after it, a "did you mean" hint (in yellow, when stderr is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset) for whichever configured name is closest by edit (Levenshtein)
distance, scaled to that name's length so an unrelated one is never
suggested \(em no external tool involved, and any single\-character typo
(dropped, inserted, or substituted) is caught, not just a dropped one.
The same hint appears for an unrecognized top\-level command.
.IP
.B \-y
(or
.BR \-\-yes )
skips the hint and acts on it automatically, but only when unambiguous:
if exactly one configured name is the closest match, that one is removed
instead; if two or more tie for closest, it falls back to the plain
error and hint, same as without the flag. A successful removal always
prints
.IR "removed '<name>'" ,
in green when stdout is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, naming whichever shim actually got removed.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback remove sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B init
Detect every installed shell (zsh, bash, fish) and set up
.B PATH
in each one, not just the current shell: for zsh/bash, the same
marker\-block injection
.B add
does; for fish, a dedicated snippet dropped into
.I ~/.config/fish/conf.d/shimback.fish
(auto\-sourced by fish at startup), unioned across repeated calls the same
way and removed outright by
.BR "uninstall \-\-full" .
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback init
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "list [" \-\-full "]"
List every configured shim as a table: name, source (or
.IR auto ),
fallback, policy, and diagnostic flag. Also available as
.BR ls .
Colored when stdout is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset.
.IP
.B \-\-full
also prints, as extra indented lines right under each shim's row,
whatever the table's columns leave out: source_args/fallback_args
(independent of policy \(em can show up for any shim), then whatever's
policy\-specific (error patterns for
.IR heuristic ,
the configured codes for
.IR exit\-code\-match ,
route args and strip\-matched\-args for
.IR route\-args ,
source/fallback route args and strip\-matched\-args for
.IR split\-args ,
each
.I from
->
.I to
pair for
.IR rewrite ,
and every route (plus its own baked\-in args) for
.IR route\-map ).
A shim with none of the above configured has nothing extra to show.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback list \-\-full
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "doctor [" fix "]"
Check the health of the whole shimback setup: whether the shim directory
exists and is on
.BR PATH ,
whether the config file parses, and, for each shim, whether its symlink is
dead, whether its fallback/source still exist and are executable, and
whether its policy has everything it needs to ever trigger. A shim added
with
.B \-\-force
whose source/fallback is still missing is reported as fine rather than
broken, for that check only, until it actually exists. It also reports the
installation recorded in the
.B PATH
block (which binary it's installed at, or that shimback isn't installed),
with a warning \(em which does not affect the exit status \(em for more than
one recorded installation (possible only from a version that predates
.BR install 's
single\-installation rule) or for a recorded directory that no longer holds a
shimback binary. Exits
.B 0
if everything checks out,
.B 1
otherwise.
.IP
.B fix
repairs what it safely can before reporting: a shim whose symlink is
missing entirely, or is dangling (its target no longer exists, e.g. because
the binary it pointed at has since moved or been cleaned up), gets its
symlink recreated pointing at the currently running
.B shimback
binary \(em the same thing
.B add
does when it first creates one. Never touches a symlink that still
resolves, or a path occupied by anything other than a symlink.
.IP
Also checks every shim's source (if explicit) and fallback for a cycle (a
value resolving back to the
.B shimback
binary itself \(em see
.BR add ,
above; the only way one ends up in the config, since
.B add
refuses to create one, is by hand\-editing it). When found,
.B fix
prompts interactively in the terminal for a replacement, re\-validating
each answer the same way
.B add
would and re\-prompting on an invalid one or another cycle, until it gets
something that works or is interrupted with
.BR Ctrl-C .
If stdin isn't interactive and hits EOF before being answered,
.B fix
gives up on that entry and leaves it as\-is rather than hang.
.IP
Also flags a stray PATH block: if
.I ~/.zshrc.local
exists but the shimback block is still in
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
that's a
.BR warn ,
not a failure \(em it doesn't affect the exit status, since PATH still
works either way.
.B fix
moves the block over.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback doctor fix
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "install [\-\-prefix " dir "] [\-\-force] [\-\-shell " shell "[," shell "]\|.\|.\|. | \-\-all]"
Copy the running
.B shimback
binary to
.IR dir /bin/shimback
(default
.IR dir :
.IR \(ti/.local )
and ensure both
.IR dir /bin
and the shim directory (normally
.BR add / init 's
job) are on
.BR PATH .
Also installs this man page to
.IR dir /share/man/man1 ,
preferring a copy bundled next to the running binary and falling back to
downloading it from the shimback GitHub release matching the running
version if no local copy is found (requires
.BR curl ;
skipped with a warning, non\-fatally, if unavailable).
.IP
Only one installation is allowed. If shimback is already installed at this
same prefix,
.B install
prints a warning and does nothing (use
.B update
to upgrade it from a release);
.B \-\-force
overwrites that installation with the running binary instead, e.g. after a
local rebuild (and is how to add
.B PATH
setup for another shell after the first install). If it's installed at a
.I different
prefix,
.B install
refuses, even with
.BR \-\-force :
run
.B uninstall
first. An installation is a directory recorded in the
.B PATH
block that really contains a shimback binary; a stale entry doesn't count.
.B install
never checks for newer releases.
.IP
By default only the
.B current
shell gets a
.B PATH
entry, like
.BR add .
.B \-\-shell
takes a comma\-separated list to target specific shells regardless of which
one is running;
.B \-\-all
means every shell
.B init
would detect as installed, fish included.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback install \-\-prefix ~/.local \-\-shell zsh,bash
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "update [\-\-check]"
Replace the installed
.B shimback
with the latest GitHub release. The installation is found from the
.B PATH
block, so a prior
.B install
is required. Downloads this platform's release archive and its
.IR SHA256SUMS ,
verifies the archive's SHA\-256 (computed natively), and refuses anything
unverifiable or mismatched without touching the installed binary; otherwise
swaps in the new binary atomically at the same path, so every shim symlink
keeps working, and refreshes the man page. Compares the downloaded binary's
contents with the installed one rather than version numbers, so a re\-tagged
release is picked up and an identical one reports "already up to date".
.B \-\-check
only reports whether an update is available. Requires
.B curl
and
.BR tar .
By default the release is fetched from the GitHub
.I latest/download
URL with
.B curl
restricted to HTTPS, including through redirects, so the trust anchor is
HTTPS to github.com, as for
.IR install.sh .
The checksums come from the same place as the archive: they catch corruption
or a mismatched pair, but do not authenticate the location itself (releases
are not signed). The environment variable
.B SHIMBACK_RELEASE_URL
overrides the release location (a mirror, or a
.I file://
directory); that is an explicit decision to trust it, and
.B update
warns each time it is used.
.RS
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
shimback update
shimback update \-\-check
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "uninstall [\-\-prefix " dir "] [\-\-full]"
Remove every symlink
.B shimback
created in the shim directory, the installed
.B shimback
binary and its man page, and the
.B PATH
setup in shell startup files (the zsh/bash marker block, and the fish
conf.d snippet). The installation is found from that
.B PATH
block, so a custom
.B \-\-prefix
given to
.B install
doesn't need repeating; pass
.B \-\-prefix
only to override the lookup (default when none is found:
.IR \(ti/.local ).
The config file is left alone (it's the user's data); with
.BR \-\-full ,
also delete it and every shim's split config file. The
.B PATH
block is kept only if another installation, possible only from an older
version, is still recorded in it.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback uninstall \-\-full
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B edit
Opens
.I config.toml
in
.BR $EDITOR ,
or, if that's unset (or empty), the first of
.BR nvim ,
.BR vim ,
.BR vi ,
.BR nano ,
.B pico
found on
.BR PATH ,
in that order. Fails with a clear error if
.B $EDITOR
isn't set and none of those five are found either. A multi\-word
.B $EDITOR
(e.g. set to
.IR "code --wait" )
works as expected \(em run through a shell so its own flags are honored,
not treated as part of a single literal command name. The config's
directory is created first if it doesn't exist yet, so this works even
before the first
.BR add .
Once the editor exits, if it exited successfully but the file it left
behind no longer parses, a warning is printed right away (without failing
the command) rather than letting the next
.BR add / list / doctor
surface a confusing error far removed from the edit that caused it.
.IP
Given a shim's
.IR name ,
opens whichever file defines that shim instead: its split config file if
it has one (wherever it currently lives among the three locations), else
.IR config.toml .
A name that isn't configured anywhere fails with a "no shim configured"
error (plus a typo suggestion if one's close) rather than opening
something unrelated. A malformed split file can still be opened this way
to fix it.
.RS
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
shimback edit
shimback edit sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B info
Prints everything shimback knows about the shim
.IR name ,
in sections: an overview (its policy and what that policy does, the
.B diagnostic
and
.B force
flags); its locations (the shim's symlink and whether it's healthy, the
config file that defines it \(em
.I config.toml
or a split file, and which of the three locations that one is in \(em a
warning if a stale
.I config.toml
entry is being ignored because a split file wins, and whether typing the
shim's name really runs the shim or another binary earlier on
.B PATH
bypasses it); its source (explicit, or
.B auto
and what that currently resolves to), fallback, and their fixed
arguments, each with a health tag; whatever drives its policy (error
patterns, exit codes, route arguments, rewrite rules, or every route of a
.BR route\-map ,
with each route's own arguments); the effective trial\-run timeout and
output limit and whether each comes from a per\-shim override, the global
config, or the default; and finally an ASCII diagram of how an invocation
flows through the shim, drawn from its real resolved paths, arguments,
routes, and thresholds. Colored when stdout is a terminal (and
.B NO_COLOR
isn't set); plain ASCII otherwise.
.IP
Read\-only and informational: the exit status is 0 for any shim it can
describe, however many problems it points out
.RB ( doctor
is the full health check). An unknown name fails with a plain "no shim
configured" error (unlike
.BR remove / edit ,
.B info
doesn't offer a typo suggestion), and a symlink with no configuration
behind it (an orphan) is reported as such and exits 1.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback info java
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH POLICIES
.TP
.B exit\-code
(the default) Fall back whenever the source command exits non\-zero.
.TP
.B heuristic
Only fall back when the source's stderr matches one of the shim's
configured
.B \-\-error\-pattern
values (case\-insensitive substring match). Requires at least one
.BR \-\-error\-pattern .
.TP
.B exit\-code\-match
Only fall back when the source exits with one of the shim's configured
.B \-\-exit\-code
values (repeatable, each 0\(en255). Any other non\-zero exit surfaces as\-is.
Requires at least one
.BR \-\-exit\-code .
.TP
.B route\-args
Not a fallback\-on\-failure policy: picks source or fallback
.I before
running either, based on whether any invocation argument exactly matches
one of the shim's configured
.B \-\-route\-arg
values (fallback if so, source if not), then runs only that one with live,
inherited stdio \(em no captured trial run, no retry on failure. Pass
.B \-\-strip\-matched\-args
to drop the matched argument(s) before forwarding the rest. Requires at
least one
.BR \-\-route\-arg .
.TP
.B split\-args
Also decides up front from the arguments alone, but two\-sided: source and
fallback each get their own set of discriminating args,
.B \-\-split\-source\-arg
and
.B \-\-split\-fallback\-arg
(each repeatable; at least one of each required). A side wins only when
.I every
one of its own args is present in the invocation (a full match, not just
any one of them). If both sides fully match at once (one side's args are a
subset of the other's), the side needing more matched args \(em the more
discriminating one \(em wins; an exact tie in count favors source. If
neither side fully matches, there is nothing to decide up front, so this
falls back to plain
.B exit\-code
behavior instead: run source, and fall back on any failure. Whichever side
wins outright runs directly with live, inherited stdio (no captured trial
run) \(em the "neither matched" case is the only one that goes through the
normal captured trial run.
.B \-\-strip\-matched\-args
works the same as for
.IR route\-args ,
removing whichever side's own args actually matched before forwarding the
rest. Requires at least one
.B \-\-split\-source\-arg
and one
.BR \-\-split\-fallback\-arg .
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback add release \-s ./build\-debug \-f ./build\-release \e
    \-\-policy split\-args \e
    \-\-split\-source\-arg \-\-debug \-\-split\-source\-arg \-g \e
    \-\-split\-fallback\-arg \-\-debug \-\-split\-fallback\-arg \-g \-\-split\-fallback\-arg \-O3
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B rewrite
Not a fallback\-on\-failure policy either, and unlike every other policy,
.B \-\-fallback
is optional and never used \(em this is an alias/argument\-macro mechanism,
not a retry mechanism. Each
.BI "\-\-rewrite " from = to
rule (repeatable) is checked against every invocation argument; a match is
replaced with
.I to
before running the source, live and inherited, no capture, no retry. A
.I to
containing spaces expands into multiple forwarded arguments; an empty
.I to
drops the matched argument. Arguments matching no rule pass through
unchanged. Requires at least one
.BR \-\-rewrite .
.RS
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
shimback add tmux \-s /usr/bin/tmux \-\-policy rewrite \-\-rewrite "all=ls"
shimback add ls \-s /bin/ls \-\-policy rewrite \-\-rewrite "\-\-full=\-ltrah"
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B route\-map
.I route\-args
generalized from a single fallback to any number of them. Like
.IR rewrite ,
.B \-\-fallback
is optional and never used. Each
.BI "\-\-route " match = command
rule (repeatable) is checked, in order, against the invocation's arguments;
the first one whose
.I match
exactly equals one of them runs
.I command
instead of the source, live and inherited, no capture, no retry. No match
at all runs the source, exactly like
.IR route\-args \(aq
own no\-match case. Unlike
.IR route\-args ,
the same command can appear in more than one route \(em the motivating case
is a version\-selecting shim.
.B \-\-strip\-matched\-args
works the same as for
.IR route\-args ,
removing whichever route's matched token before forwarding the rest. A
route can also carry its own fixed arguments \(em not settable via
.B \-\-route
itself (its
.I match=command
shape has no room for a third field without ambiguous shell quoting), but
editable directly in
.IR config.toml 's
.I args = [...]
under that route's
.I [[shims.<name>.routes]]
block \(em which is what lets the same command be reused across two routes
with different behavior.
.B add
rejects two routes that would resolve to an identical
.RI ( command ", " args )
pair \(em the second could never fire \(em both at
.B add
time and on every config load. Requires at least one
.BR \-\-route .
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback add java \-s /opt/java17/bin/java \e
    \-\-policy route\-map \e
    \-\-route "\-\-v8=/opt/java8/bin/java" \e
    \-\-route "\-\-v25=/opt/java25/bin/java"
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B passthrough
Falls back on any non\-zero exit, exactly like
.IR exit\-code ,
but skips the invisible trial run entirely \(em the source runs with live,
inherited stdio from the start, so its output (success
.I or
failure) streams to the terminal in real time instead of being held back
for a possible replay. This trades away the "invisible" property every
other fallback\-on\-failure policy has: by the time fallback runs, the
source's own failure output has already been seen. Because nothing is ever
captured or hidden under this policy,
.B \-\-diagnostic
and
.B \-\-capture\-timeout
/
.B \-\-capture\-limit
are rejected if set alongside it \(em there is nothing for any of them to
apply to.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback add terraform \-s /opt/terraform\-1.5/bin/terraform \e
    \-f /opt/terraform\-1.9/bin/terraform \e
    \-\-policy passthrough
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
By default, falling back leaves no trace. Pass
.B \-\-diagnostic
at
.B add
time (or set
.I diagnostic = true
in the config) to print a single line to stderr whenever that shim falls
back:
.RS
.nf
shimback: 'sed' failed; falling back to /usr/bin/sed
.fi
.RE
Not available for
.IR passthrough :
nothing is ever hidden under that policy, so there is nothing for a
diagnostic to report \(em
.B add
and config validation reject the combination.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/shimback/config.toml
Shim configuration (falls back to
.IR $HOME/.config/shimback/config.toml
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset). Plain, hand\-editable TOML, managed by
.B add
and
.BR remove .
A top\-level
.I "verbose = true"
sets the default for
.BR add 's
shell\-startup\-file PATH\-update notices; omitted or
.I false
keeps them silent unless
.B \-v
/
.B \-\-verbose
is passed on that particular
.BR add .
Top\-level
.I capture_timeout_ms
and
.I capture_limit
set the global defaults for the trial\-run capture cutover (see
.B DESCRIPTION
above); omitted, they default to
.I 2000
and
.IR "\(dq8MiB\(dq" .
A shim can override either with its own
.IR capture_timeout_ms / capture_limit
(see
.BR add ,
above).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/shimback/bin
The shim directory: every shim's symlink lives here (falls back to
.IR $HOME/.local/share/shimback/bin ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 127
The shim, source, or fallback couldn't be found or isn't executable.
.TP
.B 1
A CLI or config validation error.
.TP
other
Passed straight through from whichever of source/fallback actually ran (or
128 + signal if it was killed by a signal).
